    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201702047748_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Part Time Faculty Math/Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2024-04-23\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nAbout Harrisburg University Harrisburg University offers an applied science and technology education for students who want a degree and a dedicated pathway to a career. Its focus is to educate career-minded individuals in the areas of science, technology, engineering and math (STEM careers), giving them the tools to succeed, while helping to shape their communities, locally and beyond. Our goal is to help stimulate economic growth in our region by establishing a STEM educated workforce — a necessary resource in today's economy.  Mission: The Harrisburg University of Science and Technology offers innovative academic and research programs in science and technology that respond to local and global needs. The institution fosters a diverse community of learners, provides access and support to students who want to pursue a career in science and technology, and supports business creation and economic development.  ----------------------------------------------------------  We are seeking dedicated part-time instructors committed to fostering student success and engagement in our Engineering and Math Sciences program across multiple campuses, including Philadelphia and Harrisburg. The ideal candidates will have a passion for teaching first-generation students, a student-centered approach, and expertise in supporting students who may struggle with Math and Physics concepts. Additionally, candidates should be adept at offering skills-based learning opportunities and hands-on experiences in the classroom. This position will support and contribute to multiple Programs at our University.   Key Responsibilities: Create inclusive and supportive learning environments tailored to the needs of first-generation students and learners with varying mathematical and scientific backgrounds. Deliver engaging and interactive instruction in Physics and Applied Mathematics courses (including Statistics, Calculus and Differential Equations) and courses including computational skills including Python, MATLAB, R and Tableau. Implement innovative teaching strategies that emphasize skills-based learning, problem-solving, and real-world applications. Provide individualized support and guidance to students to enhance their understanding and mastery of course content. Design and facilitate hands-on activities, labs, and projects that promote experiential learning and critical thinking skills. Foster a collaborative and student-centered classroom culture that encourages participation, teamwork, and active learning. Assess student progress through a variety of methods and provide constructive feedback to support student growth and development. Specific courses needed are:   College Algebra Calculus I  Calculus II Calculus III  Differential Equations Mathematical Modeling  Introductory Statistics Predictive Modeling Advanced Analytics & reporting (Risk Analysis) University Physics I & II Engineering Mechanics: Statics Engineering Mechanics: Dynamics   Qualifications: Master's degree or higher in Physics, Mathematics, Engineering, or a related field (Ph.D. preferred). Demonstrated teaching experience at the undergraduate level, with a focus on supporting diverse student populations. Strong understanding of pedagogical approaches that promote student engagement, retention, and success. Ability to create inclusive and equitable learning environments that value diverse perspectives and backgrounds. Proficiency in designing and implementing hands-on learning experiences and skills-based assessments. Excellent communication, mentoring, and interpersonal skills to effectively engage with students and colleagues.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9200750178842_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "PT Faculty Information Systems Engineering an",
      "description": "Posted: 2023-02-03\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nAbout ISEM at HU: Harrisburg University is actively seeking a part time, adjunct faculty member with strong teaching skills for the Information Systems Engineering and Management (ISEM) Master’s Program.  The ISEM Program cuts across three active areas of work: Information Systems, Systems Engineering and Management. To accommodate teaching needs in both its Master’s and PhD programs, ISEM is currently seeking one or more faculty members with expertise in research design and methodologies.  Position Summary:  The ideal candidate is exceptionally qualified in the research process and quantitative and qualitative research methods. The candidate will be required to teach research design and methodology courses to Master's and PhD level students. This involves teaching students how to craft a research problem, perform a literature review then plan, schedule and then successfully execute a research project using classic research methods.  Specific areas of interest are as follows: Research Proposal development (GRAD 695). This is the first of the two-course Master’s students’ Capstone experience. The focus in this course is conduct of a literature review, definition of a research problem and development of a plan for execution of a successful research project using classic research methods.  Master’s Thesis development (ISEM 699, GRAD 699). This is the second of the two-course Master’s Capstone experience. In this course, students execute their GRAD 695 Research Proposal and generate their final Master’s Thesis. Research Methods (GRAD 509). This is a required course for incoming ISEM PhD students that prepares the students for PhD level research.  Specific course descriptions are available upon request. See the contact point listed below. Essential Responsibilities:  Commitment to working with diverse student populations at Master’s and PhD academic levels Commitment to equity in education at Master’s and PhD levels Commitment to delivery of quality education in accordance with defined HU, ISEM and specific course requirements Ability to periodically travel to the Harrisburg University Campus at 326 Market Street, Harrisburg, PA 17101 three Saturdays in a 15-week semester to deliver course material face-to-face to students. Qualifications: Interested candidates should have: Earned Ph.D. or equivalent is preferred. Minimum of Master’s degree is required, in an Engineering or Management discipline. Demonstrated teaching experience in Research Methods and Thesis development at the graduate and / or doctoral levels. Preferred experience with research that generates scholarly outputs including conference presentations, refereed journal publications, and other scholarly works. Willingness and aptitude for teaching and utilizing technology in the classroom. Experience working with, mentoring and advising students. Current or prior corporate / industry experience is valued. In addition to the required qualifications above, ideal candidates should have experience with learning management systems (LMS) and online teaching platforms. The required HU LMS technology is Canvas and MS Teams. Interested candidates should apply on the HU website. Specific questions can be forwarded to Dr. Robert Pittman, ISEM Academic Program Lead, at  RPittman@HarrisburgU.edu .",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=273936081089_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Part Time/Adjunct Faculty: Chemistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2020-04-06\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nAbout Harrisburg University:   \n Harrisburg University offers an applied science and technology education for students who want a degree and a dedicated pathway to a career. Its focus is to educate career-minded individuals in the areas of science, technology, engineering and math (STEM careers), giving them the tools to succeed, while helping to shape their communities, locally and beyond. Our goal is to help stimulate economic growth in our region by establishing a STEM educated workforce — a necessary resource in today's economy.   \n Mission:   \n The Harrisburg University of Science and Technology offers innovative academic and research programs in science and technology that respond to local and global needs. The institution fosters a diverse community of learners, provides access and support to students who want to pursue a career in science and technology, and supports business creation and economic development.   \n ----------------------------------------------------------   \n HU seeks a part-time/adjunct faculty member to teach our undergraduate General Chemistry lectures and labs course for this upcoming Fall 2020 semester, and beyond.   \n    \n CHEM 151 General Chemistry I Lecture   \n Description: This course provides a general introduction to atoms and molecules, stoichiometry, states of matter, solutions, reactions, kinetics and equilibrium which serve as a prerequisite for advanced courses.   \n    \n CHEM 152 General Chemistry I Laboratory   \n Description : Companion laboratory component that illustrates the general introduction to atoms and molecules, stoichiometry, states of matter, solutions, reactions, kinetics and equilibrium which serve as a prerequisite for advanced courses.   \n --------------   \n About the INSC program at HU:   \n The ABET-accredited B.S. in Computer and Information Science program at Harrisburg University of Science and Technology focuses on the science behind the coolest gadgets and gear on the market. It involves the skeletons of today’s most powerful websites, and is the root of tomorrow’s most rewarding careers. Technology at Harrisburg University is more than just computer screens and keyboards. It’s a hands-on study of the complex information systems and peripherals that power our world and shape our future.   \n All CISC undergraduate students will complete the CISC program core courses. The core courses are the program required courses and all students, regardless of their plan of study, should successfully complete all these courses. Then, students work with their academic advisors to craft an appropriate program of study which provides personal training in subject and skill areas that align with their career goals, interests, and personal strengths.   \n Accredited by ABET, our curriculum does not limit the students to a pre-defined set of concentration courses, however, a CISC student must successfully complete 15 credit hours of CISC 300 and 400-level courses with a minimum of 6 credits of 400-level courses to graduate. The offerings of the CISC program provide the students with opportunities to deepen their skills and knowledge of cognate areas in Cyber Security, Artificial Intelligence, and Software Engineering.   \n Qualifications:   \n Interested candidates should provide evidence of experience in teaching kinesthetic learners, instructional development, and assessment. Prior industry experience preferred.    \n Harrisburg University requires dedicated professionals who have the experience and desire to work in an active and collaborative environment that stresses high quality service delivery, entrepreneurial spirit, and close contact with a diverse group of internal and external constituents. HU is committed to the support and encouragement of a multicultural environment and seeks candidates who can make a positive contribution in delivering the HU Mission. _____________________________________   \n   Harrisburg University- At a Glance:   \n · Only science and technology-focused comprehensive university between Philadelphia and Pittsburgh   \n · Private, non-sectarian, nonprofit, urban, teaching university   \n · Located in the city of Harrisburg   \n · Incorporated in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania: December 12, 2001   \n · Chartered by Pennsylvania Department of Education: January 20, 2005   \n · Inaugural class arrived August 29, 2005   \n · Accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education, June 22, 2009   \n · 350 full- and part-time employees   \n · Signature 16-story, $73 million, state-of-the-art Academic Center   \n    \n Student Population:   \n · The University's total student enrollment for undergraduate and graduate degree seeking students: 6,300 as of May 1, 2019   \n · Undergraduate Student Body: 51% female, 55% minority   \n · 98% of our students demonstrate financial need   \n · Ranked in top 20 of US News & World Report’s ethnic diversity category for Best National Liberal Arts Colleges   \n · HU places 92% of its graduates in family-sustaining careers in their fields of study within six months of graduation   \n · HU undergraduate student average debt upon graduating is $7,000 less than the national average student education debt   \n    \n Academics:   \n · Full-time faculty with Ph.D. or Terminal Degree: 100%   \n · Bachelor of Science (B.S.) degrees awarded in Advanced Manufacturing- Analytics- Biotechnology and Biosciences- Computer and Information Sciences- Cybersecurity & Operations Management- Environmental Science- Interactive Media- Management, Entrepreneurship & Business Administration- Geospatial Technologies- Integrative Sciences- IS/IT & Nursing (RN-BSN)   \n · Master of Science Degrees awarded in Analytics- Biotechnology- Human Centered Interaction Design- Information Systems Engineering & Management- Learning Technologies- Computer Information Sciences- Project Management- Pharmaceutical Sciences- Techpreneurship- Disruptive Technologies & Healthcare Informatics   \n · PhD awarded in Data Analytics – first student cohort began in Fall 2017. A PhD in ISEM (Information Systems Engineering Management) started its first student cohort in Summer 2019   \n · Trimester system   \n    \n Institutional Leadership:   \n A 30-member, independent, self-perpetuating Board of Trustees led by an Executive Committee governs Harrisburg University. The Board of Trustees delegates authority for day-to-day operations to senior management.   \n    \n Notes of Distinction:   \n · First private nonprofit science and technology-focused institution chartered in the Commonwealth since Carnegie Mellon University   \n · Educational framework is competency-based, applied and experiential   \n · Offers Central Pennsylvania’s only master’s degrees in IT Project Management or Learning Technologies   \n · The University is a Project Management Institute® registered education provider   \n · Home to the Center for Advanced Entertainment and Learning Technologies, The Government Technology Institute, and The Geospatial Technology Center   \n · Co-PI on NSF-funded ADVANCE grant focused on the recruitment, retention, and development of women academics in science, technology, engineering and mathematics (STEM) disciplines   \n    \n Institutional Affiliations & Partnerships:   \n · American Council on Education (ACE)   \n · Association of Independent Colleges and Universities of Pennsylvania (AICUP)   \n · Coalition of Urban and Metropolitan Universities (CUMU)   \n · National Science Foundation (NSF)   \n · Technology Council of Central Pennsylvania (TCCP)   \n · Consortium agreements with:   \n - Penn State University – USA   \n - St. Francis University – USA   \n - Ramnarian Ruia College - Mumbai, India   \n - Acharya Institutes - Bangalore, India   \n - Universidad Rey Juan Carlos - Madrid, Spain   \n - The University College of the Cayman Islands - Grand Cayman, Cayman Islands   \n - EFREI, Engineering School of Information and Digital Technologies and Esigetel, Digital Systems and Communicating Objects – Paris, France   \n - Universitat Internacional de Catalunya- Barcelona, Spain   \n - Rede das Faculdades de Tecnología e Ciencias - Bahía, Brazil   \n - St. Aloysius College- India   \n - Dayananda Sagar Institutions - Bangalore, India   \n · Articulation agreements with:   \n - Harrisburg Area Community College   \n - Community College of Baltimore County   \n - Lehigh Carbon Community College   \n - Alvernia University   \n - Montgomery College   \n - Community College of Philadelphia",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204283352954_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor - Inpatient Physical Therapy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nPOSITION SUMMARY The Harrisburg University DPT program is a hybrid 3-year program. This core faculty position holds a faculty appointment at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, commensurate with experience, and carries academic, service, clinical, and scholarship responsibilities consistent with the mission and philosophy of the HU DPT program. Under the general direction of the DPT Program Lead, core faculty in this position are responsible for curriculum and content development in inpatient physical therapy, with advanced clinical expertise in either cardiovascular and pulmonary physical therapy or neurologic physical therapy, and for routine review and revision of course content to ensure consistency with contemporary evidence-based practice patterns in these areas. Primary teaching responsibility for this position includes inpatient and acute care physical therapy content and cardiovascular and pulmonary and/or neurologic physical therapy; the core faculty member will also contribute to additional courses and integrated curricular experiences based on individual expertise and program needs. The HU DPT Program uses a hybrid educational model that combines online learning with intensive, hands-on laboratory immersion experiences. Core faculty work collaboratively to develop and deliver integrated course content, facilitate laboratory experiences, assess student performance, and support students throughout the curriculum, and are expected to teach effectively in both virtual and in-person environments, including intensive laboratory experiences at the Harrisburg campus. The core faculty member will cultivate each learner’s professional growth and will participate in coaching/mentoring learners. They will engage in scholarship activities, with priority for those which engage and enhance the academic and professional development of students. They are required to maintain competence in the academic field by remaining current in their own and related disciplines and in the most effective hybrid instructional methods. They will demonstrate effectiveness in the synthesis, interpretation, and communication of new knowledge. They will demonstrate experience in teaching, curriculum development, course coordination, student advising, teamwork, and scholarship. They will participate in service to HU and to the profession of physical therapy. They will assist and support the faculty team in all CAPTE accreditation activities.  In addition to instructional and scholarly activity, this individual will participate in activities consistent with the policies of Harrisburg University. This is a full-time, 12-month faculty position with instructional terms in spring, summer, and fall. Regular travel to the state of Pennsylvania is required. Participation in DPT program lab immersions is required. __________________________________________________________________________________________ AREAS OF CLINICAL EXPERTISE This position requires expertise in cardiovascular and pulmonary physical therapy or neurologic physical therapy, combined with significant inpatient clinical experience. Additional experience in one or more of the following areas is desirable: Acute care physical therapy Intensive and critical care Inpatient rehabilitation Cardiovascular and pulmonary rehabilitation Neurologic rehabilitation Medically complex patient management Patient monitoring and physiologic response to activity Laboratory values and clinical decision-making Acute and chronic disease management Hospital-based mobility and functional training Medical equipment, lines, tubes, and monitoring systems Interprofessional healthcare practice Care transitions and discharge planning __________________________________________________________________________________________ KEY ACCOUNTABILITIES The duties of this core faculty position include teaching, clinical, research and service responsibilities. The overall determination of research and clinical activities and allocation of time and effort will be subject to the determination of the Program Lead. Develop, deliver, and routinely assess and refine course content within area of clinical expertise (cardiovascular and pulmonary and/or neurologic physical therapy), and contribute to inpatient and acute care physical therapy content throughout the curriculum, integrating contemporary clinical practice, current evidence, clinical practice guidelines, and healthcare standards. Ensure the scope, depth, and breadth of course content, and maintenance of current records of student participation and grades, comply with program and CAPTE standards. Develop and administer effective student learning assessments, including written and practical examinations, skills assessments, case-based assignments, and other competency-based assessments, to determine achievement of student learning outcomes in classroom and/or laboratory settings. Collaborate with faculty in a co-teaching model to integrate content across courses and curricular areas, and participate actively in on-campus laboratory immersions and other required in-person DPT educational experiences. Provide learner coaching consistent with the HU DPT learner coaching model, including meeting with assigned students to review academic progress, establish goals, identify opportunities for development, and connect students with appropriate resources; participate in student remediation and academic support processes as appropriate. Provide training and mentoring for course and lab assistants. Establish and maintain a scholarly agenda consistent with area of expertise and Harrisburg University expectations, and disseminate scholarly work through peer-reviewed publications, professional presentations, research activities, educational scholarship, or other appropriate scholarly products. Participate in DPT programmatic activities to support program development, assessment, accreditation, and maintenance of a high-quality DPT program, including curriculum mapping, program outcome assessment, faculty evaluation, and continuous quality improvement. Provide support for CAPTE self-study and annual accreditation activities, student ceremonies, DPT faculty committees, and faculty search committees, and assist with preparation and maintenance of accreditation reports, self-study documentation, and other required program materials. Contribute to student recruitment, admissions, orientation, and other program activities, and establish and maintain collaborative relationships with clinical partners and members of the physical therapy and healthcare communities. Attendance at DPT meetings and educational events is expected. Participate in faculty development in area of content expertise, hybrid course delivery, and virtual reality and immersive technology. Contribute to an environment that promotes growth, equity, and freedom of speech. __________________________________________________________________________________________ EXPECTATIONS OF ALL STAFF Applies best efforts and full capability each day to the work assigned by their manager.    Advises the manager when:  An assignment is not understood.    An assignment conflicts with knowledge of the circumstances, current skills, or available time/resources.    Obstacles to completing the assignment exist or are likely to occur.    Opportunities to better complete or improve the assignment exist or may occur.    Understands and demonstrates our cultural expectations. __________________________________________________________________________________________ MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Earned Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT) degree from a CAPTE-accredited physical therapist education program. DPT with ABPTS Board Certified Clinical Specialization preferred. Terminal academic degree preferred (i.e., PhD, EdD, DSc, DHSc). Active, unrestricted Pennsylvania Physical Therapy license, or eligibility to obtain Pennsylvania licensure prior to initiating teaching responsibilities. Minimum of three years of full-time post-licensure clinical experience, including significant experience in inpatient physical therapy. Demonstrated clinical expertise in either cardiovascular and pulmonary physical therapy or neurologic physical therapy. Minimum of two years’ experience teaching or in curriculum development in a physical therapy education program, or teaching experience in a clinical, professional, or higher education setting. Demonstrated ability to translate contemporary clinical evidence and practice into entry-level DPT education. Effective communication, organizational, and interpersonal skills. Ability to work cooperatively within a faculty team, including in a collaborative, co-teaching model. Ability to teach effectively in both online and intensive in-person laboratory environments. Understanding of higher education and contemporary clinical practice appropriate for teaching in physical therapy education. Adherence to professional standards, including the APTA Core Values and Code of Ethics. Knowledge of legislative, regulatory, legal, and practice issues affecting students and the profession of physical therapy. Strong commitment to inclusivity and diversity, and ability to interact effectively with diverse cultural and ethnic groups. Knowledge, skill, and ability to effectively demonstrate and provide accurate and constructive feedback to DPT learners regarding professional behaviors, affective skills and abilities, and clinical skills. Ability to facilitate learner acquisition of the skills and abilities required for entry-level clinical practice. Active in professional activities at local, state, and/or national levels. Commitment to student learning, professional development, and student success. Preferred Qualifications: Academic experience in a DPT entry-level educational program specific to inpatient, cardiovascular and pulmonary, neurologic, acute care, or medical management content. ABPTS Board Certification as a Cardiovascular and Pulmonary Clinical Specialist (CCS) or Neurologic Clinical Specialist (NCS). Clinical experience across multiple inpatient settings, including acute care, critical care, inpatient rehabilitation, long-term acute care, or other hospital-based environments. Knowledge and experience in a hybrid educational model, or a development plan to improve skillset in distance learning/hybrid instruction. Experience facilitating intensive laboratory, simulation, or immersive learning experiences. Demonstrated evidence of teaching effectiveness and desire to implement new and current technologies in both distance and onsite educational environments. Experience with collaborative or team-based course development and co-teaching. Demonstrated innovation, organization, problem solving, leadership, conflict resolution, and interpersonal skills in areas related to professional practice. Evidence of an established or developing scholarly agenda. Experience mentoring, advising, or coaching graduate or professional students. _________________________________________________________________________________________ WORK HOURS The university work week is Sunday through Saturday. Most administrative offices are open Monday through Friday from 8:00 am to 5:00 pm. To accommodate the college's business needs, employees may be assigned to other workdays and/or hours, including weekends and evenings. Employees may be required to work overtime. Please speak to your direct supervisor regarding your position-specific expected schedule.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204253046301_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "DPT Program & Clinical Education Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nPOSITION SUMMARY The DPT Program and Clinical Education Coordinator is responsible for independently coordinating key administrative and operational functions of the DPT (Doctor of Physical Therapy) Program, with particular responsibility for clinical education, student compliance, laboratory operations, program processes, and accreditation-related documentation. Reporting to the DPT Program Lead and working closely with the Director of Clinical Education (DCE), the Coordinator exercises independent judgment in prioritizing program needs, resolving operational issues, coordinating with internal and external stakeholders, monitoring compliance requirements, and recommending improvements to program processes. The position serves as a key operational liaison among DPT leadership, faculty, students, clinical education partners, University departments, vendors, and other stakeholders. The Coordinator is expected to independently manage assigned areas of responsibility within established University and program policies and to identify matters requiring escalation to program leadership. __________________________________________________________________________________________ KEY ACCOUNTABILITIES Clinical Education Coordination Independently coordinate administrative processes supporting clinical education activities and clinical site relationships. Coordinate clinical affiliation agreements and related documentation, identify outstanding requirements, and work with University personnel and clinical partners to facilitate timely completion. Review and reconcile clinical affiliation agreements in collaboration with the Director of Clinical Education and legal counsel. Monitor student clinical education compliance requirements, including health documentation, immunizations, clearances, and other required materials. Review compliance information and determine when students have outstanding requirements that may affect eligibility for clinical education activities, referring exceptions or significant concerns to the DCE or Program Lead as appropriate. Manage clinical education information within Exxat and other designated systems and ensure the accuracy, completeness, and timeliness of program records. Serve as a primary administrative liaison with clinical sites regarding onboarding requirements, documentation, deadlines, and clinical education processes. Identify and independently resolve routine clinical education operational issues and recommend solutions for more complex matters to the DCE. Monitor clinical education timelines, student requirements, timesheets, and assignment completion and initiate appropriate follow-up when requirements are not met. Coordinate clinical education orientation activities and communications with students, Clinical Instructors, and clinical partners. Analyze recurring clinical education administrative issues and recommend improvements to processes, communication, and workflow. Program Operations and Administration Coordinate day-to-day administrative operations of the DPT Program within assigned areas of responsibility. Independently prioritize competing operational needs and deadlines based on program requirements, academic schedules, accreditation expectations, and student needs. Serve as a primary point of contact for students, faculty, University departments, visitors, vendors, and external partners regarding assigned program operations. Evaluate inquiries and operational concerns, determine appropriate courses of action within established policies, and escalate matters requiring faculty or leadership decisions. Coordinate program calendars, meetings, events, and other operational activities requiring collaboration among multiple internal and external stakeholders. Prepare and coordinate agendas, meeting materials, minutes, reports, correspondence, and follow-up activities. Monitor program expenditures, purchasing activities, and budget information within assigned areas and identify discrepancies or concerns for program leadership. Coordinate purchasing and vendor relationships for program equipment, supplies, services, and laboratory needs. Maintain program records and develop processes to support compliance with University policies, FERPA requirements, CAPTE accreditation requirements, and other applicable standards. Support preparation and organization of documentation for accreditation, assessment, program review, and other regulatory or institutional requirements. Evaluate existing administrative workflows and recommend and implement approved improvements to increase program efficiency and effectiveness. Independent Judgement and Decision-Making The Coordinator is expected to exercise discretion and independent judgment in carrying out assigned responsibilities. This includes: Establishing priorities among competing program and clinical education operational needs. Evaluating information and determining appropriate administrative actions within established University and program policies. Resolving routine operational and clinical education coordination issues without direct supervision. Determining when issues present significant compliance, student progression, operational, or partnership concerns requiring escalation. Recommending modifications to administrative procedures and workflows. Coordinating across University departments and external organizations to accomplish program objectives. Evaluating laboratory operational needs and initiating appropriate actions within established authority. Handling confidential and sensitive information requiring judgment regarding appropriate communication and access. __________________________________________________________________________________________ EXPECTATIONS OF ALL STAFF Applies best efforts and full capability each day to the work assigned by their manager.    Advises the manager when:  An assignment is not understood.    An assignment conflicts with knowledge of the circumstances, current skills, or available time/resources.    Obstacles to completing the assignment exist or are likely to occur.    Opportunities to better complete or improve the assignment exist or may occur.    Understands and demonstrates our cultural expectations. __________________________________________________________________________________________ MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. Minimum of three years of progressively responsible experience in program coordination, higher education administration, healthcare administration, clinical education, or a related setting. Demonstrated ability to independently manage multiple projects, priorities, and deadlines. Demonstrated experience exercising judgment and resolving operational problems. Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to work effectively with faculty, students, administrators, healthcare professionals, external partners, and vendors. Demonstrated ability to manage confidential and sensitive information. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, including Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, and Teams. Ability to learn and manage specialized databases and program management systems. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in higher education, healthcare education, or clinical education. Experience supporting an accredited healthcare professional education program. Familiarity with Exxat or a comparable clinical education management platform. Experience with accreditation documentation, regulatory compliance, or program assessment. Experience coordinating relationships with healthcare organizations, clinical sites, or external partners. Experience with budget monitoring, purchasing, vendor management, or operational planning. _________________________________________________________________________________________ WORK HOURS The university work week is Sunday through Saturday. Most administrative offices are open Monday through Friday from 8:00 am to 5:00 pm. To accommodate the college's business needs, employees may be assigned to other workdays and/or hours, including weekends and evenings. Employees may be required to work overtime. This particular role requires an on-campus presence of 2-3 days per week, coordinated with program needs, and will need to be on campus some evenings and weekends for lab dates.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204189760920_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Human Resources Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-27\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nPOSITION SUMMARY ** This is a hybrid role requiring 3 days in the office per week. ** The Human Resources Coordinator provides transactional and administrative support across the HR department, handling the processing, recordkeeping, and coordination work that keeps HR operations running accurately and on schedule. Reporting to the CHRO, this role carries an expanded share of the department's day-to-day workload, including administrative recruiting support for hiring committees now that recruiting is coordinated through committee-based search processes rather than a dedicated recruiter. The HR Coordinator is the first point of contact for routine HR inquiries and supports benefits administration, onboarding and offboarding, and employee records, escalating matters requiring judgment or discretion to the HR Manager and/or CHRO. __________________________________________________________________________________________ KEY ACCOUNTABILITIES HRIS and Recordkeeping Maintain accurate employee records in ADP Workforce Now, including new hire entry, status changes, and terminations. Maintain physical and electronic personnel files in accordance with legal, regulatory, and institutional standards. Compile data and prepare standard HR reports as directed by the HR Manager or CHRO. Benefits Administration Support Oversee administration of employee benefits programs, including health, dental, vision, retirement, and leave. Serve as the first point of contact for routine benefits inquiries; escalate complex situations to the HR Manager. Support open enrollment through employee communications, data entry, and HR Manager coordination. Onboarding and Offboarding Coordinate new hire administrative onboarding, including offer letter preparation, background check initiation, I-9 completion, and paperwork collection. Facilitate orientation logistics, including scheduling, materials, and system access coordination with IT and other departments. Administer exit interview program; analyze trends and present recommendations to the CHRO. Support offboarding by preparing separation paperwork and ensuring timely completion of required documentation. Recruiting Support Provide administrative recruiting support as directed by the HR Manager, including postings, interview scheduling with hiring committee members, candidate communications, and applicant tracking maintenance. Employee Engagement Support Support the HR Manager in planning and executing engagement events, including logistics, vendor communication, materials, and on-site coordination. Track participation and gather feedback from engagement activities to support reporting and program evaluation. General HR Support Respond to routine HR inquiries from employees and managers; triage and escalate as appropriate. Assist with compliance tasks including I-9 audits, EEO data collection, and other required reporting under the direction of the HR Manager. Support preparation of HR communications, forms, and departmental materials as directed. Provide general administrative support to the HR department, including document management and other tasks as assigned.  ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ EXPECTATIONS OF ALL STAFF Applies best efforts and full capability each day to the work assigned by their manager.    Advises the manager when:  An assignment is not understood.    An assignment conflicts with knowledge of the circumstances, current skills, or available time/resources.    Obstacles to completing the assignment exist or are likely to occur.    Opportunities to better complete or improve the assignment exist or may occur.    Understands and demonstrates our cultural expectations. _____________________________________________________________________________________________________________ MINIMUM ROLE REQUIREMENTS Associate's degree required; Bachelor's degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, or a related field preferred. One to two years of administrative or HR support experience preferred. Proficiency in Microsoft Office, including Word, Excel, and Outlook. Experience with or aptitude for HRIS systems; familiarity with ADP Workforce Now a plus. Strong attention to detail and commitment to data accuracy. Excellent organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple tasks and deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Strong interpersonal and communication skills with a professional and approachable demeanor. __________________________________________________________________________________________ WORK HOURS The university work week is Sunday through Saturday. Employees are expected to work within the core hours of 8:00 am to 5:00 pm, Monday through Friday. To accommodate business needs, employees may be assigned to other workdays and/or hours, including weekends and evenings, and may be required to work overtime.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=d7d41d6e-e9f2-43e0-af0f-ac2146083d4f&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9204284337982_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Academic Records & Work Placement Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\n\nLocation: Harrisburg\n\nPOSITION SUMMARY The Academic Records and Work Placement Coordinator administratively supports academic programs and students by managing courses, maintaining student records, and facilitating communication among stakeholders.  In addition, this role is accountable for managing and supporting the work placement requirements of students, ensuring compliance with university policies, and facilitating communication between students, faculty, and relevant university offices.   __________________________________________________________________________________________ KEY ACCOUNTABILITIES Communicates policies and procedures to students, faculty, and administration.  Develops term schedules and creates courses in the student information system (SIS).  Support students with registration and manage all related enrollment processes.  Record timely information to student records accurately and in accordance with institutional policies, and tracks degree requirements.  Support students in all student system processes and related procedures from matriculation to graduation.  Create and maintain procedures in alignment with Records and Registration policies and institutional academic operations guidelines.  Manage support request submissions and associated forms and processes for students, including document storage, and develop academic calendars and timelines for recurring communication.  Manage the collection, submission, and maintenance of student work placement documentation to ensure accuracy and completeness.  Evaluate work placement requests for alignment with academic programs, collaborating with program leads to determine approvals.  Communicate work placement requirements and provide related guidance to students.  Complete the final step in processing project and internship contracts by verifying documentation, updating student records, and ensuring registration for the associated class. Manage educational and credential verification services on behalf of the University Registrar, including verification and authentication of official transcripts, diplomas, degrees, and other academic documents; prepare and issue documents bearing the University seal and authorized signatures; coordinate notarial services and support state and international authentication/Apostille requirements while maintaining the security, confidentiality, and integrity of official university records. __________________________________________________________________________________________ EXPECTATIONS OF ALL STAFF Applies best efforts and full capability each day to the work assigned by their manager.    Advises the manager when:  An assignment is not understood.    An assignment conflicts with knowledge of the circumstances, current skills, or available time/resources.    Obstacles to completing the assignment exist or are likely to occur.    Opportunities to better complete or improve the assignment exist or may occur.    Understands and demonstrates our cultural expectations. ________________________________________________________________________________________ MINIMUM ROLE REQUIREMENTS Bachelor's degree or equivalent work experience.  Two years of higher education experience preferred.  Excellent computer skills, including knowledge of Microsoft Office, spreadsheets, and document merging.  Strong verbal and written communication skills, customer service, record keeping, and attention to detail.  Ability to work independently, think ahead, multitask, and possess excellent time management and organizational skills.  General understanding of federal, state, and local regulations as they apply to student records (i.e., FERPA, HIPAA, PDE, etc.) and best practices related to academic policies and procedures.  Proficiency in Windows and Microsoft Office computer systems, student information systems, online student support tools, and learning-management systems and workflows. Ability to manage online student records and documentation in compliance with FERPA and institutional confidentiality standards. __________________________________________________________________________________________ WORK HOURS The university work week is Sunday through Saturday. Most employees are expected to work within the core hours of 8:00 am to 5:00 pm, Monday through Friday. To accommodate business needs, employees may be assigned to other workdays and/or hours, including weekends and evenings, and may be required to work overtime.",
